<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ferrante I Gonzaga</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1507">1507</a>–<a href="/years/1557">1557</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-gonzaga">House of Gonzaga</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ferrante I Gonzaga was a son of Isabella d'Este and a leading general of the Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>, serving as Viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and from 1546 as governor of the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>. In 1539 he bought the county of Guastalla from Countess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludovica-torelli">Ludovica Torelli</a>, founding the Gonzaga line that ruled it, raised to a duchy under his grandson Ferrante II in 1621.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ferrante-i-gonzaga.jpg" alt="Ferrante I Gonzaga" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fermo Guisoni · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ritratto_di_Ferrante_I_Gonzaga_(Ghisoni).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ferrante was born in Mantua on 28 January 1507, the third son of Francesco II Gonzaga and Isabella d'Este. At sixteen he was sent to the Spanish court as a page to the future emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>, to whom he stayed loyal all his life. In 1529 he married Isabella di Capua, who brought him the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a> of Molfetta and Giovinazzo and made him one of the great feudal lords of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>. Their children included Cesare, who succeeded him, and two cardinals, Francesco and Gian Vincenzo.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Ferrante took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sack-of-rome-1527">Sack of Rome</a> in 1527 and attended Charles's coronation at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> in 1530. He defended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> against the French under Odet of Foix, Viscount of Lautrec, and won the surrender of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, for which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-clement-vii">Pope Clement VII</a> made him papal governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a>. He became a Knight of the Golden Fleece in 1531, fought the Turks at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a> in 1535 and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> in 1541, and served as Viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> from 1535 to 1546.</p><p>In 1543 he was ambassador to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII of England</a> and went with the emperor to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, fighting the campaign that enforced the Treaty of Crépy. As governor of the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> from 1546 to 1554 he fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-parma">War of Parma</a>. The county of Guastalla, bought for 22,280 golden scudi, made him a sovereign prince of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, and it lay near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a>, which Charles wished to take from the Este.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ferrante rebuilt his villa near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, La Gualtiera, now La Simonetta, in the 1550s withthe Tuscan architect Domenico Giuntallodi of Prato. He protected the sculptor Leone Leoni, whose medal of about 1555 shows Hercules defeating the Nemean Lion, alluding to Ferrante's acquittal of charges of misappropriation and corruption; Cesare later commissioned Leoni's bronze Triumph of Ferrante Gonzaga over Envy for Guastalla. He died in 1557 after a fall from his horse at the Battle of St. Quentin and was buried in Mantua Cathedral.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ferrante Gonzaga” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pier Luigi tried to curb the local nobility, and on 10 September 1547 he was murdered in Piacenza by conspirators acting with the support of <b>Ferrante Gonzaga</b>, the imperial governor of Milan, whose troops at once occupied the city.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-parma-and-piacenza">Duchy of Parma and Piacenza · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1539 Countess Ludovica Torelli sold Guastalla to <b>Ferrante Gonzaga</b>, general of Charles V and governor of Milan, whose branch of the Gonzaga ruled it from then on.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-guastalla">Duchy of Guastalla</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Mantua. Died in Brussels metropolitan area, in what is now <a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.85,4.35&amp;year=1539" aria-label="Brussels metropolitan area on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4649 400 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Ferrante I Gonzaga"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5099 670h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5099 670h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=50.85,4.35&amp;year=1539">What was here in 1539? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
